          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is it ever correct to submit a code root without its decimal characters?",
          "answer": "Yes, in some cases. The retrieved guidance says a code only has a decimal when more characters follow the first three. For example, essential hypertension is I10, which has no decimal because it is complete at three characters (*Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*; *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*).\n\nMost codes do need more characters. The guidance says most ICD-10-CM categories require a fourth, fifth or sixth character before the code is complete. Type 2 diabetes codes, for example, are never used at three characters (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*). So a bare three-character root is valid only when that category is complete on its own.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not cover billing or payer rules, so they cannot tell you how a particular claim system treats a code. For that, check the official ICD-10-CM tabular list or ask a qualified medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
